@*
    One group of rows in a task's detail pane, under the word that says what they
    have in common.

    The group is what the pane lays out, not the row. A pane that put its rows
    straight into columns would break "due date, reminder, repeat" across the gap
    and stand a due date next to a priority, which reads as a relationship
    neither of them has. Grouping first is what makes the columns mean something:
    a column is a subject, so the reader picks the subject and then the row
    instead of scanning every row for the one they wanted.

    The caption is not drawn, and the label is still required.

    Those two facts are one decision. On screen the grouping is already doing the
    work: the rows of a group sit together, the group never breaks across the
    column gap, and there is a gap between one group and the next — so a reader
    can see which rows belong together without a word over them. Three or four
    captions in a pane of eight short rows spends a line each on saying what the
    spacing has already said, and they were the loudest text in the pane while
    naming the least interesting thing in it.

    What a caption is genuinely for is the reader who cannot see the layout. A
    screen reader gets no grouping from proximity, so the group is still named,
    still by this string, and still through aria-labelledby rather than a repeated
    aria-label — the same words twice is the same words twice to a screen reader.
    That is why the parameter stays required: a group with no name is a group only
    half the audience can perceive.

    A paragraph rather than a heading, for when it is read: a heading would have to
    guess its own level, and a panel does not know how deep in a page it is.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" role="group" aria-labelledby="@_captionId" data-testid="@TestId">
    <p class="task-action-group__caption sr-only" id="@_captionId">@Label</p>
    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the rows in it have in common — "Scheduling", "Ranking".
    /// One word where one will do.
    /// <para>
    /// Required although it is not drawn. On screen the grouping is carried by the
    /// layout; this is the group's accessible name, which is the only grouping a
    /// reader who cannot see that layout gets.
    /// </para></summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The rows. <see cref="TaskAction"/> in every case so far, though
    /// nothing here requires it — a group is a labelled column, and what goes in
    /// a column is the pane's business.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private readonly string _captionId = "task-action-group-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    private string ClassValue =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "task-action-group" : $"task-action-group {CssClass}";
}
